<title>BELGIUM: BELGIAN RENAULT WORKERS TAKE PROTEST TO PM.</title>
<headline>BELGIAN RENAULT WORKERS TAKE PROTEST TO PM.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-03-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>Workers from Belgium's doomed Renault car plant, fresh from taking part in Sunday's massive march for jobs in Brussels, took their protest action to the prime minister's front door on Monday.</p>
<p>Belga news agency reported that some 300 workers from the plant at Vilvoorde -- where Prime Minister Jean-Luc Dehaene lives -- turned up on his doorstep in a fleet of coaches shortly after lunch.</p>
<p>No one was at home. But Celie Dehaene, the prime minister's wife, arrived soon after with a police escort from an outside visit.</p>
<p>The workers handed her pamphlets and stickers and left after an hour. There were no incidents.</p>
</text>
